walkthrough "windows 11 - install to a desktop"
  resolution 1920x1080
  recording_resolution 1920x1080
  user vmuser
  password admin
  var boot_timeout == {default: "1800", from: args.boot_timeout}
  var computer_name == {default: sandbox, from: args.computer_name}
  var out == {default: out/windows11.qcow2, from: args.out}
  var user == {default: vmuser, from: args.user}
  var windows_image_index == {default: "6", from: args.windows_image_index}
  header ssh_port == 2222

  needs windows.iso virtio-win.iso, named "the media this stage boots from is already on disk"
  step "create the empty disk"
    disk == disk.qcow2
    disk_gb == 128
  step "build the answer CD"
    fetch == answer_iso
    template == windows/11/answer/Autounattend.xml
  step "boot the installer"
    answer_cd == answer.iso
    boot == true
    driver_cd == virtio-win.iso
    iso == windows.iso
    os == windows
    # FOUR GIBABYTES IS THE ENGINE'S DEFAULT AND IT IS NOT ENOUGH FOR WHAT WINDOWS DOES AFTER THE
    # INSTALL. MEASURED across two runs that both sat on the same frame for the whole budget:
    #
    #	Please keep your computer on. Your computer may restart a few times.
    #
    # with `Select C:\WINDOWS\SYSTEM32\netsh.exe` over it, `ratio=0.1x` and `sat=+0.07` - a screen
    # that never changed for 90 and then 120 minutes. The console log shows the boot loader handing
    # over to Windows Boot Manager and nothing after it, so the guest is running and its first-boot
    # work is not finishing.
    #
    # EIGHT IS WHAT THIS GUEST'S OWN STAGES ASK FOR: `ssh` and `vm-configure` both boot with the
    # default and only need sshd, while the install is the one that runs the OOBE.
    memory_mb == 12288
    timeout == 1800
  step "wait for the firmware to offer the DVD"
    blind_after == 60
    timeout == 180
    until == DVD-ROM
    from == console
  press Escape, waiting 1s, named "press a key so the loader takes it"
  press Escape, waiting 1s, named "press it again, because the prompt is emitted more than once"
  press Escape, waiting 2s, named "and once more, for a loader that was not listening yet"
  step "Setup is on screen"
    loop == {check: {from: screen, mode: text, name: done, words: [Windows, Setup], rect: [0, 0, 1920, 1080], rect_at: [1920, 1080]}, do: [{settle: 5}], max: 180, see: {name: screen}, until: {fact: done}}
  # NINETY MINUTES WAS NOT ENOUGH ON A GITHUB RUNNER, AND THE FRAME SAYS WHY.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44377939172, which spent its whole 1h30m and failed:
  #
  #   wait for the desktop: no windows desktop within 1h30m0s; drop=+20.7 (need 15)
  #     ratio=0.1x (need 2) sat=+0.07 (need 0.10) on a 1920x1080 frame
  #
  # THAT FRAME IS "Please keep your computer on. Your computer may restart a few times." - the
  # installer's own OOBE, still posting. `desktop: windows` is RIGHT to refuse it: a black screen with
  # one line of white text has no variance and no saturation, which is exactly the two measurements
  # that failed.
  #
  # SO THE BUDGET IS THE THING THAT WAS WRONG, not the predicate. Two hours is the same order as the
  # install above it (`timeout: 1800` for the boot, then this), and a leg whose guest is slow loses
  # the whole run to a number chosen before one had been measured on this runner.
  # THE OOBE STOPS DEAD ON ITS OWN REBOOT NOTICE UNLESS SOMETHING TOUCHES THE KEYBOARD.
  #
  # MEASURED TWICE. Run 44377939172 spent its whole 1h30m and run 44410380340 its whole 2h on the
  # same frame:
  #
  #	Please keep your computer on.
  #	Your computer may restart a few times.
  #
  # with a `Select C:\WINDOWS\SYSTEM32\netsh.exe` window over it. The measurements the predicate
  # uses are on that frame - ratio=0.1x and sat=+0.07, both under what a desktop needs - so
  # `desktop: windows` was RIGHT every time it refused, for two hours.
  #
  # `desktop:` IS A PASSIVE POLL: it takes a frame and decides, and nothing in it presses a key. The
  # OOBE waits for one. So the keys come first, in a loop that is bounded and ends when the screen is
  # no longer the notice - `until: {fact: ...}` is not usable here (no mode reports it), so the loop
  # is a fixed number of nudges and `desktop:` still has the real timeout.
  # THE PRESSES ARE FURTHER APART AND THERE ARE FEWER OF THEM, BECAUSE THE FIRST SET MEASURED NO
  # EFFECT. Ten Returns twenty seconds apart, then two hours of the same frame: `netsh.exe` is
  # sysprep's own window and it does not read the keyboard. A key pressed into a busy sysprep is at
  # best ignored and at worst queued into whatever runs next.
  #
  # SO THEY ARE A GENTLE PERIODIC NUDGE - a minute apart, six of them - for the case where the OOBE
  # really is waiting on the "press any key" line, and the memory above is the actual fix for the
  # stall that was measured.
  #
  # AND SIX RETURNS INTO A RUNNING INSTALL IS SIX CHANCES TO PRESS SOMETHING THAT IS NOT A NUDGE.
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44468093359, whose recording ends on this exact frame after 2h07m:
  #
  #	Installing Windows 11        54% complete
  #	[ Windows 11 Setup ]  Are you sure you want to quit?   [Yes]  [No]
  #
  # The installer had reached 54% and was working, and the sixth Return landed on its `Cancel`
  # button, which carries the FOCUS on that page. The "are you sure" dialog then sat there until
  # the budget ran out. The nudge was written for an OOBE that waits on "press any key", and NOTHING
  # IN IT COULD TELL THAT SCREEN FROM AN INSTALL THAT IS 54% DONE.
  #
  # AND THE ANSWER IS GATED ON THE DIALOG ITSELF, NOT ON THE PROGRESS LINE.
  #
  # THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS GATE WAS `when_not: [complete]` AND IT FAILED FOR A REASON THAT IS IN
  # THE ENGINE: `doUnless` treats an unreadable screen as an ERROR, because "the words are absent"
  # and "nobody looked" are different facts. MEASURED ON RUN 44490615000, which died at step 10:
  #
  #	nudge the first boot along, once - unless: 0 word(s):
  #	status: failed step 10/22 "nudge the first boot along, once"
  #
  # The band was the progress line and the screen at that moment was "We're getting a few things
  # ready", which has none. So the gate refused, and a guard against cancelling an install became a
  # way to lose one.
  #
  # `when:` READS, AND A MISSING READING IS A SKIP. That is the behaviour this needs: press the key
  # ONLY when the quit dialog is on screen, and do nothing at all on every other frame.
  #
  # WHY THE DIALOG AND NOT "press any key": MEASURED on the 2h07m recording, where the progress line
  # reads 28% at 200 s, 54% at 600 s, and 54% again at 1200, 1800, 2400 and 3000 - the install had
  # stopped, and the quit dialog is up from about 520 s. The dialog is what has to be answered.
  step "answer a quit dialog, if one is up"
    key == Escape
    rect == [820, 460, 1520, 660]
    rect_at == [1920, 1080]
    # SCALE 2, BECAUSE SCALE 1 DOES NOT READ THE WORD THIS GATE NEEDS. Read back from the recording:
    # scale 1 gives "yeusureyouwantto au?" - no "quit" in it, so the gate would never open - and
    # scale 2 gives "Are you sure you want to quit?". Escape is not Enter: on this page the focused
    # control is `Cancel`, and Escape does not activate a focused button.
    scale == 2
    settle == 15
    when == [quit]
  # THE SAME ANSWER ONCE MORE, because a dialog that has been dismissed can come back: the first
  # one appeared only because a stray Return had pressed Cancel, and no Return is sent any more.
  step "answer it again, if it came back"
    key == Escape
    rect == [820, 460, 1520, 660]
    rect_at == [1920, 1080]
    scale == 2
    settle == 15
    when == [quit]
  # THE DESKTOP IS DECIDED BY THE REFERENCE IMAGE, NOT BY THREE BRIGHTNESS THRESHOLDS.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THIS GUEST'S OWN RECORDINGS. `desktop: windows` wanted drop>=15, ratio>=2x and
  # sat>=0.10 - three numbers within a few percent of each other - and the OOBE frames failed them
  # with `ratio=0.1x sat=+0.07` for TWO HOURS while the console log showed Windows Boot Manager had
  # started.
  #
  # THE SAME QUESTION, ASKED WITH A REFERENCE, SEPARATES CLEANLY. Measured on one ubuntu recording
  # frame by frame against the reference that file ships:
  #
  #	 30s   similarity 0.0004   boot splash
  #	 90s   similarity 0.0041   still splashing
  #	120s   similarity 0.9909   the desktop
  #	1600s  similarity 0.9767   the desktop, later
  #
  # "not a desktop" tops out at 0.0043 and "a desktop" bottoms out at 0.9669 - two orders of
  # magnitude - where the thresholds gave 0.1x against a floor of 2.
  #
  # THE REFERENCE IS THE ONE THE `check:` BELOW ALREADY USES, so there is one picture of this desktop
  # in the repository rather than two that could drift apart.
  step "wait for the desktop"
    desktop == windows
    reference == reference/desktop.png
    rect == [0, 0, 1920, 120]
    rect_at == [1920, 1080]
    range == [0.90, 1.0]
    timeout == 7200
  # THE DESKTOP IS THE PICTURE, NOT ONLY THE NUMBERS. `desktop:` decides on seven measurements -
  # brightness drop, variance ratio, saturation - and several panes can share all seven. This step
  # says the screen is THE ONE THIS FILE WAS MEASURED ON, by comparing pixels to a frame taken from
  # this repository's own recording of this leg.
  #
  # 3% IS THE CEILING AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY: the same screen a second later differs by 0.5-0.9%,
  # and two different screens by 98-100%, so the gap is three orders of magnitude wide.
  # A FIRST LOGIN LEAVES THINGS OPEN THAT A DESKTOP DOES NOT HAVE LATER.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44341011692, which failed here at 0.7092 similar with the reference at 0.9700:
  # the frame the walk captured had the START MENU open and a "Screen resolution" notification in the
  # corner, and the reference - taken from this same leg's own recording - was the clean desktop.
  # `desktop: windows` had passed because it decides on brightness, variance and saturation, and a
  # menu over a wallpaper moves none of them past its thresholds.
  #
  # SO THE SCREEN IS SETTLED BEFORE IT IS COMPARED, which is the same reason every later step in this
  # file starts with an Escape: the desktop is the state, the menu is the leftover.
  # A CLICK ON THE WALLPAPER, NOT ONLY AN ESCAPE. Escape closes the START PANEL; the notification
  # in the corner is a separate window and the click is what dismisses it - measured on the frame
  # this comparison rejected, where both were up at once.
  click "the desktop, to dismiss anything a first login left open" at 960 540, waiting 3s, named "click the empty desktop"
  press Escape, waiting 2s, named "and an Escape in case a panel was the thing that took it"
  # A NOTIFICATION IS NOT DISMISSED BY A CLICK ON THE WALLPAPER. MEASURED ON RUN 44377939172, after
  # the click and the Escape had been added: 0.9088 similar, and the frame is a clean Windows desktop
  # with ONE "Settings / Searching for Display Driver" panel in the bottom-right corner. The click
  # went to the desktop; a toast belongs to the shell.
  #
  # IT EXPIRES ON ITS OWN in about thirty seconds, which is what this settles for. Waiting is the
  # honest fix: the alternative is a reference that contains a transient toast, and then the compare
  # is measuring how fast the runner is rather than whether the desktop came up.
  settle 45s
  step "the desktop is the one this file was written against"
    see == shot
  step "the screen is the one this file was written against"
    # THE TASKBAR IS EXCLUDED, BECAUSE EVERY RUN PUTS DIFFERENT ICONS IN IT.
    #
    # MEASURED: two clean Windows desktops from two runs score 0.9555 over the whole frame and
    # 0.9999 over everything above the taskbar. The wall paper, the two desktop icons and the
    # notification area are identical; the PINNED ICONS AND THE CLOCK ARE NOT - one run had Firefox
    # and Explorer pinned, the other did not, and the clocks read 9:52 and 11:13.
    #
    # A COMPARISON THAT FAILS ON A CHANGING CLOCK IS MEASURING THE CLOCK. What this step is for is
    # "the desktop came up and it is this system", and the region above the taskbar says exactly that
    # while the taskbar says what was pinned at the time.
    #
    # `rect` as a PIXEL pair with `rect_at`, rather than a fraction, because 1000/1080 is the edge of
    # a taskbar that is 40 px tall at this resolution - a fraction would move with the guest's size
    # and the taskbar's edge is what has to be excluded.
    check == {name: matched, must: true, from: shot, mode: like, rect: [0, 0, 1920, 1000], rect_at: [1920, 1080], reference: reference/desktop.png, range: [0.97, 1.0]}
  step "reboot into the installed system"
    boot == true
    os == windows
    timeout == 900
  step "the installed system reaches a desktop"
    desktop == windows
    timeout == 1800
  step "shut the installed system down"
    acpi == power
    timeout == 300
  step "wait for the machine to stop"
    wait_until == stopped
    timeout == 300
  step "publish the disk"
    compress == '{{ out }}'
